{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB11866 - MONDO_8159",
  "verbalization": "Romosozumab is implicated in the treatment of postmenopausal osteoporosis through its indications for osteoporosis and its downstream relationships with specific drugs that target different mechanisms in the pathophysiology of the condition. The evidence suggests that Romosozumab may exert its therapeutic effect by influencing bone metabolism, as it is indicated for osteoporosis, which in turn is linked to postmenopausal osteoporosis. The presence of multiple downstream pathways—such as interactions with Ipriflavone, Minodronic Acid, and Bazedoxifene—indicates that Romosozumab may act through a common or complementary mechanism in the regulation of bone density and mineral homeostasis. These drugs are known to modulate bone resorption and formation, suggesting that Romosozumab may similarly influence these processes. The integrated evidence supports a mechanistic link between Romosozumab and postmenopausal osteoporosis, though the exact mode of action remains to be fully elucidated.",
